 up by the  oceans his powers cannot be fully  measured but in  many stories he has shown himself to be  more than formidable  added to this neil latham is immortal  his antics will continue  for as long as he chooses and humans are  only his slaves and  toys and on the other hand we have  cthulhu  who is lovecraft's most famous creation  and hence the entire mythology is also  named after  him cathedral is inspired by the  writer's own hydrophobia  nylathotep's home is outside the known  limits of space but cathulis abode is  earth itself  specifically relia which is at the  bottom of the pacific ocean  he represents that part of the unknown  which is closer to us and yet  which we have not fully been able to  explore the darkness of the ocean and  this creature is representative of the  dangerous elements that are closer to us  but which we have only a vague sense of  this malevolent entity is classified as  belonging to the rank of the great old  ones  and this character first appeared in the  call of cthulhu where he is portrayed as  a horrifying creature who sleeps under  the ocean  in the city of arlier he has a great  cult of worshippers who await his  liberation  it is believed that once he rises from  the depths he will rule over the earth  and cause chaos by stripping men off  their sense of right and wrong  through him they will learn the euphoria  that comes with immorality  and take a dionysian or bacchanalian  revelry in the act of killing  a new holocaust will emerge unlike in  the case of nair lathotep  lovecraft does give us some definite  details about cthulhu's grotesque form  he is a chimera monster in the sense  that his body is composed of parts that  are not  usually seen as belonging together his  body is a combination of an octopus  with tentacles growing from the chin a  dragon fish like scales  and human parts he also has a pair of  basic and crude  wings that kinda look draconian while  many artists and writers since lovecraft  have come 